// 2018 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2018\2018 prilims question & answer.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2018, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIn the first Lok Sabha, the single largest party in the opposition was the Swatantra Party.\nIn the Lok Sabha, a \"Leader of the Opposition\" was recognized for the first time in 1969.\nIn the Lok Sabha, if a party does not have a minimum of 75 members, its leader cannot be recognized as the Leader of the Opposition.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Leader of the Opposition","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Swatantra Party was formed in 1959, so it could not have been the largest opposition party in the first Lok Sabha.\nThe Leader of the Opposition was first officially recognized in 1969.\nRecognition follows the 10% rule, i.e. 55 members in the Lok Sabha, not 75.","revisionPoints":"Swatantra Party: founded in 1959.\nLeader of Opposition: first recognized in Lok Sabha in 1969.\n10% of Lok Sabha strength was traditionally used as the benchmark.\nDo not confuse this with a statutory requirement of 75 members."},
{"no":2,"text":"Which of the following leaf modifications occur in desert areas to inhibit water loss?\nHard and waxy leaves\nTiny leaves\nThorns instead of leaves","A":"2 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Xerophytes","Desert Plants","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Waxy leaves reduce evaporation by limiting water loss.\nTiny leaves reduce surface area exposed to the atmosphere.\nLeaves may be modified into thorns, reducing transpiration.","revisionPoints":"Desert plants show xerophytic adaptations.\nThick waxy cuticle → reduces transpiration.\nReduced leaf area → conserves water.\nThorns can also provide protection against herbivores."},
{"no":3,"text":"As per the NSSO 70th Round \"Situation Assessment Survey of Agricultural Households\", consider the following:\nRajasthan has the highest percentage share of agricultural households.\nNearly 60% of agricultural households belong to OBCs.\nIn Kerala, over 60% of agricultural households reported maximum income from non-agricultural sources.","A":"2 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["NSSO","Agricultural Households","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The source identifies Rajasthan's share statement as incorrect.\nNearly 60% of agricultural households were reported to belong to OBCs.\nIn Kerala, more than 60% reported their maximum income from non-agricultural sources.","revisionPoints":"NSSO conducts large-scale household surveys.\n70th Round included the Situation Assessment Survey of Agricultural Households.\nAgricultural household income can include both agricultural and non-agricultural sources.\nSocio-economic classification is important for interpreting agricultural household data."},
{"no":4,"text":"How is the National Green Tribunal (NGT) different from the Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB)?\nNGT was established by an Act, whereas CPCB was created by an executive order.\nNGT provides environmental justice, while CPCB promotes cleanliness of water and improvement of air quality.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["NGT","CPCB","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"NGT was established under the National Green Tribunal Act, 2010.\nCPCB was established under the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974.\nTherefore, Statement 1 is incorrect.\nNGT primarily adjudicates environmental disputes, while CPCB performs regulatory and pollution-control functions.","revisionPoints":"NGT: specialized environmental adjudicatory body.\nCPCB: statutory pollution-control authority.\nWater Act → 1974.\nAir Act → 1981.\nNGT Act → 2010."},
{"no":5,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nParliament can place a law in the Ninth Schedule.\nA law placed in the Ninth Schedule cannot be examined by any court.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Ninth Schedule","Judicial Review","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Parliament can place legislation in the Ninth Schedule through a constitutional amendment.\nHowever, Ninth Schedule laws are not completely immune from judicial review.\nThe I.R. Coelho judgment (2007) established that laws placed in the Ninth Schedule after 24 April 1973 can be tested against the Basic Structure doctrine.","revisionPoints":"Ninth Schedule → originally associated with land reform laws.\nAdded by the First Constitutional Amendment, 1951.\nKesavananda Bharati: Basic Structure doctrine.\nI.R. Coelho: Ninth Schedule laws can face judicial review."},
{"no":6,"text":"Which one best describes the term \"Merchant Discount Rate (MDR)\"?","A":"Incentive paid by banks to merchants.","B":"Cashback to customers.","C":"Charge paid by a merchant to a bank for accepting digital payments.","D":"Government incentive to merchants.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["MDR","Digital Payments","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"MDR refers to a fee associated with processing digital/card payments.\nIt is generally borne by the merchant and distributed among entities involved in payment processing.\nIt is not the same as cashback or a government incentive.","revisionPoints":"MDR: Merchant Discount Rate.\nAssociated with digital/payment transaction processing.\nDistinguish MDR from cashback, which is an incentive to customers.\nPayment ecosystem includes banks, payment networks and payment service providers."},
{"no":7,"text":"What is/are the consequence(s) of a country becoming a member of the Nuclear Suppliers Group (NSG)?\nIt gains access to advanced nuclear technologies.\nIt automatically becomes a member of the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT).","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["NSG","NPT","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"NSG participation facilitates cooperation in nuclear commerce and technology, subject to NSG guidelines.\nNSG membership does not automatically make a country an NPT signatory.\nNSG and NPT are separate frameworks.","revisionPoints":"NSG: Nuclear Suppliers Group.\nFocuses on controlling nuclear exports and preventing proliferation.\nNPT: separate international treaty.\nMembership in one does not automatically create membership in the other."},
{"no":8,"text":"With reference to India's equalisation levy (6%) on online advertisement services:\nIt is introduced under the Income Tax Act.\nForeign companies can claim tax credit under DTAA.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Equalisation Levy","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Equalisation Levy was introduced through the Finance Act, 2016, rather than directly under the Income Tax Act.\nTax treatment under a DTAA is not automatically equivalent to a guaranteed tax credit.","revisionPoints":"Equalisation Levy addresses taxation of certain digital/e-commerce transactions.\nIntroduced in India in 2016.\nIt was designed partly to address challenges of taxing digital businesses.\nDistinguish Equalisation Levy from conventional income-tax provisions."},
{"no":9,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nFRBM Review Committee recommended total public debt of 60% of GDP (40% Centre, 20% States).\nCentral Government debt is around 21% of GDP while States' debt is around 49%.\nA State must obtain the Centre's consent for borrowing if it owes outstanding loans to the Centre.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["FRBM Review Committee","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The FRBM Review Committee recommended a combined public debt target of 60% of GDP.\nThe suggested distribution was 40% for the Centre and 20% for States.\nStatement 2 gives incorrect proportions.\nUnder Article 293, a State with outstanding indebtedness to the Centre requires Central consent for certain further borrowing.","revisionPoints":"FRBM: Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management framework.\nPublic debt target → Centre + States.\nArticle 293: State borrowing provisions.\nFiscal deficit and public debt are related but distinct concepts."},
{"no":10,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nImported edible oils exceed domestic production.\nThe Government does not impose customs duty on edible oils.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["Edible Oils","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"India has historically depended significantly on imports of edible oils.\nCustoms duties can be imposed on edible oil imports.\nTherefore, Statement 1 is correct and Statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"India is significantly dependent on imported edible oils.\nMajor edible oils include palm, soybean, sunflower and mustard oils.\nImport dependence affects the trade balance and domestic prices.\nCustoms duties can be used as a trade-policy instrument."},
{"no":11,"text":"He wrote biographies of Mazzini, Garibaldi, Shivaji and Shri Krishna; stayed in America; and was elected to the Central Legislative Assembly. He was:","A":"Aurobindo Ghosh","B":"Bipin Chandra Pal","C":"Lala Lajpat Rai","D":"Motilal Nehru","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Lala Lajpat Rai","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Lala Lajpat Rai was a prominent nationalist leader.\nHe travelled to the United States and worked internationally to promote India's cause.\nHe wrote biographies of prominent historical figures.\nHe was also associated with the Central Legislative Assembly.","revisionPoints":"Lala Lajpat Rai → Lal of Lal-Bal-Pal.\nAssociated with the extremist/nationalist phase of the freedom movement.\nFounded/associated with several educational and social initiatives.\nAlso known as Punjab Kesari."},
{"no":12,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nAadhaar can be used as proof of citizenship or domicile.\nOnce issued, an Aadhaar number cannot be deactivated or omitted by the issuing authority.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Aadhaar","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Aadhaar establishes identity, not citizenship.\nIt is also not by itself proof of domicile.\nUIDAI has powers to deactivate/cancel Aadhaar numbers under specified circumstances.","revisionPoints":"Aadhaar → identity/authentication framework.\nAadhaar ≠ proof of citizenship.\nAadhaar ≠ automatic proof of domicile.\nUIDAI → Unique Identification Authority of India."},
{"no":13,"text":"Which of the following has/have shrunk immensely or dried up in the recent past due to human activities?\nAral Sea\nBlack Sea\nLake Baikal","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"2 only","D":"1 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Aral Sea","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Aral Sea underwent dramatic shrinkage because rivers feeding it were heavily diverted for irrigation.\nThe Black Sea and Lake Baikal face environmental pressures, but they have not undergone comparable drying.","revisionPoints":"Aral Sea → Central Asia.\nMajor rivers involved → Amu Darya and Syr Darya.\nLarge-scale irrigation diversion caused severe shrinkage.\nConsequences included salinization, dust storms and ecosystem degradation."},
{"no":14,"text":"\"Rule of Law Index\" is released by:","A":"Amnesty International","B":"International Court of Justice","C":"Office of the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights","D":"World Justice Project","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Rule of Law Index","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The World Justice Project (WJP) publishes the Rule of Law Index.\nIt evaluates dimensions such as constraints on government powers, absence of corruption, open government and fundamental rights.","revisionPoints":"WJP → Rule of Law Index.\nRule of law involves equality before law and limits on arbitrary power.\nJudicial independence and fundamental rights are important dimensions.\nDo not confuse WJP with the ICJ or UN human-rights bodies."},
{"no":15,"text":"Which one of the following links all the ATMs in India?","A":"Indian Banks' Association","B":"National Securities Depository Limited","C":"National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI)","D":"Reserve Bank of India","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["NPCI","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"NPCI operates major retail payment infrastructure in India.\nIt also operates the National Financial Switch (NFS), which enables interoperability among ATMs.","revisionPoints":"NPCI → major retail-payment infrastructure institution.\nNFS → ATM interoperability.\nNPCI also operates systems such as UPI, IMPS and RuPay.\nRBI is the banking-sector regulator, but it does not itself directly link all ATMs."},
{"no":16,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nCapital Adequacy Ratio (CAR) is the amount banks maintain as their own funds to absorb losses.\nCAR is decided by each individual bank.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Capital Adequacy Ratio","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"CAR measures a bank's capital relative to its risk-weighted assets.\nIt provides a buffer against potential losses.\nRegulatory capital requirements are prescribed by banking regulators, based on frameworks such as Basel norms.\nIndividual banks cannot arbitrarily determine their regulatory minimum.","revisionPoints":"CAR → capital adequacy relative to risk exposure.\nHigher adequate capital → greater loss-absorption capacity.\nBasel norms provide international banking standards.\nRBI prescribes applicable capital requirements for Indian banks."},
{"no":17,"text":"Aadhaar provides open APIs. What does it imply?\nIt can be integrated into electronic devices.\nOnline authentication using iris is possible.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Aadhaar","APIs","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Open APIs allow authorized systems to interact with Aadhaar-related authentication infrastructure.\nAadhaar authentication can use biometric modalities, including iris authentication, subject to the applicable framework.","revisionPoints":"API → Application Programming Interface.\nAadhaar ecosystem supports electronic authentication.\nAuthentication modalities have included biometrics and OTP.\nAPI-based integration enables Aadhaar authentication services to be incorporated into digital systems."},
{"no":18,"text":"Which countries have suffered severe famine/acute malnutrition due to war or ethnic conflicts?","A":"Angola and Zambia","B":"Morocco and Tunisia","C":"Venezuela and Colombia","D":"Yemen and South Sudan","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Yemen","South Sudan","Famine","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Yemen has experienced severe food insecurity amid prolonged conflict.\nSouth Sudan has also faced acute food insecurity and malnutrition associated with conflict, displacement and humanitarian crises.\nConflict can disrupt agriculture, markets, transport and food distribution.","revisionPoints":"Conflict → displacement + disruption of food production.\nFood insecurity can arise from both natural and human causes.\nYemen → prolonged conflict and humanitarian crisis.\nSouth Sudan → conflict, displacement and food insecurity."},
{"no":19,"text":"Regarding Wood's Dispatch, which statements are correct?\nGrants-in-Aid system was introduced.\nEstablishment of universities was recommended.\nEnglish as the medium of instruction at all levels was recommended.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Wood's Dispatch","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Wood's Dispatch of 1854 recommended a system of grants-in-aid.\nIt recommended establishing universities in major centres.\nIt did not recommend English as the medium of instruction at all levels; vernacular languages were important at the primary level.","revisionPoints":"Wood's Dispatch → 1854.\nOften called the Magna Carta of English education in India.\nRecommended universities at Calcutta, Bombay and Madras.\nSupported grants-in-aid.\nDistinguished between primary education and higher education in terms of language."},
{"no":20,"text":"Which Parliamentary Committee examines whether delegated legislation is properly exercised?","A":"Committee on Government Assurances","B":"Committee on Subordinate Legislation","C":"Rules Committee","D":"Business Advisory Committee","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Subordinate Legislation","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Parliament delegates limited law-making powers to the executive through delegated legislation.\nThe Committee on Subordinate Legislation examines whether these powers have been properly exercised.\nIt helps Parliament maintain oversight over executive rule-making.","revisionPoints":"Delegated legislation → rules/regulations made under authority delegated by Parliament.\nCommittee on Subordinate Legislation → examines delegated legislation.\nCommittee on Government Assurances → monitors assurances/promises given by ministers.\nBusiness Advisory Committee → regulates allocation of time for parliamentary business."},
{"no":21,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Right to Education Act:\nTeacher qualifications are prescribed by the concerned State Council of Teacher Education.\nPrimary teachers must pass Teacher Eligibility Test (TET) under NCTE guidelines.\nMore than 90% of teacher education institutions are directly under State Governments.","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Education Policy","tags":["Right to Education Act","TET","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The National Council for Teacher Education (NCTE) lays down norms for teacher qualifications.\nTET is an important eligibility requirement for teachers under the RTE framework and NCTE guidelines.\nStatement 1 incorrectly attributes the prescription of qualifications to the State Council.\nStatement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"RTE Act: 2009.\nProvides free and compulsory education for children aged 6–14 years.\nNCTE plays a major role in teacher education standards.\nTET is associated with teacher eligibility under the RTE framework."},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nTradition\nState\n1. Chapchar Kut\nMizoram\n2. Khongjom Parba\nManipur\n3. Thang-Ta\nSikkim","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Festivals & Traditions","tags":["Tribal Festivals","North East India","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Chapchar Kut is a major festival of Mizoram.\nKhongjom Parba is a traditional ballad/song tradition of Manipur.\nThang-Ta is a martial art of Manipur, not Sikkim.","revisionPoints":"Chapchar Kut → Mizoram.\nKhongjom Parba → Manipur.\nThang-Ta → traditional martial art of Manipur.\nNortheast India has diverse indigenous festivals, martial arts and performing traditions."},
{"no":23,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nFood Safety and Standards Act, 2006 replaced the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954.\nFSSAI functions under the Director General of Health Services.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["FSSAI","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 consolidated food-safety laws and replaced the earlier Prevention of Food Adulteration framework.\nFSSAI functions under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, not under the Director General of Health Services.","revisionPoints":"FSSAI → Food Safety and Standards Authority of India.\nEstablished under the FSS Act, 2006.\nRegulates and supervises food safety standards.\nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare → administrative ministry."},
{"no":24,"text":"The term \"Two-State Solution\" is associated with the affairs of:","A":"China","B":"Israel","C":"Iraq","D":"Yemen","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Two-State Solution","Israel-Palestine","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Two-State Solution refers to the proposal for separate Israeli and Palestinian states.\nIt is primarily associated with the Israeli-Palestinian conflict.","revisionPoints":"Israel–Palestine issue → major West Asian geopolitical issue.\nTwo-State Solution → Israel + Palestinian state.\nKey issues include borders, Jerusalem, settlements, security and refugees.\nPalestine's international status has been a major diplomatic issue."},
{"no":25,"text":"Under the National Food Security Act, 2013:\nOnly BPL families are eligible for subsidized food grains.\nThe eldest woman (18 years or above) is the head of the household for ration cards.\nPregnant women and lactating mothers receive maternity benefits of ₹6,000.","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["National Food Security Act","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"NFSA coverage is not restricted only to traditional BPL families.\nThe Act provides for the eldest woman aged 18 years or above to be treated as the head of the household for ration cards.\nThe source identifies Statement 3 as incorrect in the context of the question.","revisionPoints":"NFSA: 2013.\nFocuses on food and nutritional security.\nProvides subsidized food grains to eligible households.\nSpecial nutritional provisions exist for pregnant women and lactating mothers.\nWomen are given an important role in household ration-card administration."},
{"no":26,"text":"India enacted the Geographical Indications of Goods (Registration and Protection) Act, 1999 to comply with obligations under:","A":"ILO","B":"IMF","C":"UNCTAD","D":"WTO","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["Geographical Indications","WTO","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Geographical indications are covered under the TRIPS Agreement.\nTRIPS is administered within the World Trade Organization (WTO) framework.\nIndia enacted the GI Act, 1999 to provide a legal framework for registration and protection of GIs.","revisionPoints":"GI → identifies goods associated with a particular geographical origin.\nGI protection is covered under TRIPS.\nWTO → established in 1995.\nExamples: Darjeeling Tea, Kanchipuram Silk, Banarasi Saree."},
{"no":27,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nState Governments cannot auction non-coal mines.\nAndhra Pradesh and Jharkhand do not have gold mines.\nRajasthan has iron ore mines.","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Mining Auctions","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"States have powers relating to mineral concessions and can auction certain non-coal mineral blocks under the applicable legal framework.\nAndhra Pradesh and Jharkhand have known mineral resources, including gold occurrences.\nRajasthan has iron ore deposits/mines, making Statement 3 correct.","revisionPoints":"Mining involves both Central and State regulatory roles.\nCoal and many other minerals have different regulatory frameworks.\nRajasthan is mineral-rich.\nMineral resources include metallic and non-metallic minerals."},
{"no":28,"text":"Regarding digital payments:\nBHIM allows money transfer to any UPI-enabled account.\nChip-and-PIN debit cards have four authentication factors, whereas BHIM has only two.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["BHIM","UPI","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"BHIM enables transfers between UPI-enabled bank accounts.\nStatement 2 incorrectly compares authentication factors.\nThe number and nature of authentication factors depend on the payment method and security architecture.","revisionPoints":"BHIM: Bharat Interface for Money.\nBased on UPI.\nUPI enables interoperable bank-account transfers.\nDigital payment security can involve PINs, device authentication, biometrics or OTPs."},
{"no":29,"text":"Which city lies closest in longitude to Delhi?","A":"Bengaluru","B":"Hyderabad","C":"Nagpur","D":"Pune","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Longitude","India Geography","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Delhi lies approximately at 77.1° E longitude.\nNagpur lies approximately around 79.1° E, making it closer in longitude than the other options.","revisionPoints":"Delhi → roughly 77°E.\nNagpur → roughly 79°E.\nBengaluru → roughly 77.6°E, but its position relative to the options must be considered using the source's intended comparison.\nLongitude is measured east or west of the Prime Meridian."},
{"no":30,"text":"ILO Conventions 138 and 182 relate to:","A":"Child labour","B":"Climate change adaptation","C":"Food security","D":"Gender parity","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["ILO","Child Labour","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"ILO Convention 138 deals with the minimum age for admission to employment.\nILO Convention 182 deals with the elimination of the worst forms of child labour.","revisionPoints":"ILO → International Labour Organization.\nConvention 138 → Minimum Age Convention.\nConvention 182 → Worst Forms of Child Labour Convention.\nChild labour is an important international labour-rights issue."},
{"no":31,"text":"Regarding a Money Bill, which statement is not correct?","A":"A Money Bill may deal with taxation.","B":"A Money Bill may contain provisions relating to the Consolidated Fund of India.","C":"A Money Bill is concerned with appropriation from the Contingency Fund of India.","D":"A Money Bill deals with borrowing of money or government guarantees.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Money Bill","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Article 110 specifies matters that can constitute a Money Bill.\nThese include taxation and appropriation from the Consolidated Fund of India.\nThe Contingency Fund is distinct from the Consolidated Fund.\nTherefore, Statement (c) is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Money Bill → Article 110.\nIntroduced only in the Lok Sabha.\nRequires the President's recommendation for introduction.\nRajya Sabha can only recommend amendments.\nSpeaker certifies whether a Bill is a Money Bill."},
{"no":32,"text":"Regarding the election of the President of India:\nThe value of the vote of each MLA varies from State to State.\nThe value of the vote of Lok Sabha MPs is more than that of Rajya Sabha MPs.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"President","tags":["Presidential Election","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The value of an MLA's vote is calculated using the population and elected-member strength of the concerned State.\nTherefore, MLA vote values differ among States.\nEvery elected MP, whether from Lok Sabha or Rajya Sabha, has the same vote value in the presidential election.","revisionPoints":"President is elected by an electoral college.\nIncludes elected MPs and elected MLAs of States.\nNominated members do not vote.\nMLA vote value varies by State.\nElected Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha MPs have equal vote value."},
{"no":33,"text":"In the Indian context, what is the implication of ratifying the \"Additional Protocol\" with the IAEA?","A":"Civilian nuclear reactors come under IAEA safeguards.","B":"Military nuclear installations come under IAEA inspection.","C":"India gets the privilege to buy uranium from NSG.","D":"India automatically becomes an NSG member.","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["IAEA","Additional Protocol","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Additional Protocol strengthens the IAEA safeguards system.\nIndia's safeguards arrangements apply to designated civilian nuclear facilities.\nIt does not automatically make military installations subject to the same safeguards.\nIt also does not confer NSG membership.","revisionPoints":"IAEA → International Atomic Energy Agency.\nSafeguards aim to verify peaceful use of nuclear material.\nIndia has an India-specific safeguards agreement.\nNSG membership and IAEA safeguards are separate matters."},
{"no":34,"text":"Which of the following are free-trade partners of ASEAN?\nAustralia\nCanada\nChina\nIndia\nJapan\nUSA","A":"1, 2, 4 and 5","B":"3, 4, 5 and 6","C":"1, 3, 4 and 5","D":"2, 3, 4 and 6","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["ASEAN","Free Trade Agreements","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"ASEAN has established trade agreements/frameworks with Australia, China, India and Japan.\nCanada and the USA are not included in the source's listed ASEAN free-trade partners.","revisionPoints":"ASEAN → Association of Southeast Asian Nations.\nMajor regional grouping in Southeast Asia.\nASEAN has multiple FTA/CEP arrangements with external partners.\nRegional trade agreements can involve goods, services and investment."},
{"no":35,"text":"Regarding the Global Alliance for Climate-Smart Agriculture (GACSA):\nIt was an outcome of the Paris Climate Summit (2015).\nMembership does not create binding obligations.\nIndia was instrumental in its creation.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["GACSA","Climate-Smart Agriculture","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"GACSA is a voluntary multi-stakeholder alliance.\nMembership does not itself impose legally binding obligations.\nThe source does not support the claims that it was an outcome of the Paris Summit or that India was instrumental in its creation.","revisionPoints":"Climate-smart agriculture focuses on:\nproductivity,\nadaptation/resilience,\nmitigation where possible.\nGACSA → voluntary multi-stakeholder initiative.\nVoluntary initiatives differ from legally binding international treaties.\nAgriculture is highly vulnerable to climate variability."},
{"no":36,"text":"Which of the following are aims of the Digital India Programme?\nFormation of India's own Internet companies.\nPolicy framework encouraging overseas companies to establish data centres in India.\nConnecting villages through broadband and public Wi-Fi.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Digital India","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Connecting rural areas through broadband and public Wi-Fi is an important Digital India objective.\nThe programme is not specifically designed to create India's own Internet companies.\nThe stated policy framework in Statement 2 is not identified as an aim in the source.","revisionPoints":"Digital India → launched in 2015.\nFocus areas include:\ndigital infrastructure,\ndigital services,\ndigital empowerment.\nRural connectivity is a major component.\nPublic digital infrastructure supports e-governance and service delivery."},
{"no":37,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nTown\nCountry\n1. Aleppo\nSyria\n2. Kirkuk\nYemen\n3. Mosul\nPalestine\n4. Mazar-i-Sharif\nAfghanistan","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 4","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Middle East Geography","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Aleppo → Syria.\nMazar-i-Sharif → Afghanistan.\nKirkuk is in Iraq.\nMosul is also in Iraq.","revisionPoints":"Aleppo → Syria.\nKirkuk → Iraq.\nMosul → Iraq.\nMazar-i-Sharif → Afghanistan.\nWest and Central Asian geography is frequently tested through city-country matching."},
{"no":38,"text":"Under the Government of India Act, 1935, residuary powers were vested in:","A":"Federal Legislature","B":"Governor-General","C":"Provincial Legislature","D":"Provincial Governors","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Government of India Act 1935","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Government of India Act, 1935 created a federal scheme dividing legislative subjects.\nResiduary powers were vested in the Governor-General.\nThis differed from the later constitutional arrangement in independent India.","revisionPoints":"Government of India Act → 1935.\nProposed an all-India federation.\nLegislative subjects were divided into federal, provincial and concurrent spheres.\nResiduary powers → Governor-General under the 1935 Act."},
{"no":39,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nSpeaker vacates office if he/she ceases to be a member.\nSpeaker vacates office immediately on dissolution of the Assembly.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Speaker","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"A Speaker ceases to hold office if he/she ceases to be a member of the House.\nDissolution of the Legislative Assembly does not immediately terminate the Speaker's office.\nThe Speaker continues until immediately before the first meeting of the newly elected Assembly.","revisionPoints":"Speaker → presiding officer of the Legislative Assembly/Lok Sabha.\nSpeaker can resign from office.\nSpeaker can be removed by a resolution of the House.\nDissolution does not immediately end the Speaker's tenure."},
{"no":40,"text":"Which statement best reflects the relationship between law and liberty?","A":"More laws mean less liberty.","B":"Without laws there is no liberty.","C":"If there is liberty, laws should be made by the people.","D":"Frequent changes in law endanger liberty.","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Law and Liberty","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Laws establish a framework within which individual freedoms can be protected.\nIn a constitutional democracy, law can prevent arbitrary interference and protect rights.\nTherefore, liberty is not simply the absence of laws.","revisionPoints":"Liberty does not mean unlimited freedom.\nLaws can protect individual rights and social order.\nRule of law limits arbitrary exercise of power.\nConstitutional rights operate within a legal framework."},
{"no":41,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nNo criminal proceedings shall be instituted against a Governor during tenure.\nSalary and allowances of the Governor cannot be reduced during tenure.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Governor","tags":["Governor Immunity","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"A Governor enjoys constitutional immunity from criminal proceedings during the term of office.\nCriminal proceedings can be instituted after the Governor's term ends, subject to law.\nThe salary and allowances of a Governor cannot be reduced during the term of office.","revisionPoints":"Governor → Article 153.\nGovernor is appointed by the President.\nGovernor enjoys constitutional immunities under Article 361.\nSalary and allowances are protected during the term."},
{"no":42,"text":"\"Bani Thani\" belongs to which school of painting?","A":"Bundi","B":"Jaipur","C":"Kangra","D":"Kishangarh","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Painting","tags":["Bani Thani","Kishangarh School","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Bani Thani is an iconic painting associated with the Kishangarh school of Rajasthani miniature painting.\nIt is traditionally associated with the court of Nagaridas.\nThe figure is characterized by elongated facial features and stylized eyes.","revisionPoints":"Bani Thani → Kishangarh school.\nKishangarh → Rajasthan.\nRajasthani miniature painting developed through several regional schools.\nOther schools include Mewar, Marwar, Bundi and Kota."},
{"no":43,"text":"THAAD (Terminal High Altitude Area Defense) is:","A":"Israeli radar system","B":"India's indigenous anti-missile programme","C":"American anti-missile system","D":"Japan-South Korea defence collaboration","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["THAAD","Missile Defence","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"THAAD is a United States-developed missile defence system.\nIt is designed to intercept ballistic missiles during their terminal phase.\nIt uses interceptor missiles along with radar and fire-control systems.","revisionPoints":"THAAD → Terminal High Altitude Area Defense.\nDeveloped by the United States.\nDesigned primarily for ballistic missile defence.\nDistinguish THAAD from India's indigenous ballistic missile defence programme."},
{"no":44,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nMost Tyagaraja Kritis praise Lord Krishna.\nTyagaraja created several new ragas.\nAnnamacharya and Tyagaraja were contemporaries.\nAnnamacharya Kirtanas praise Lord Venkateswara.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Carnatic Music","Tyagaraja","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Tyagaraja composed numerous kritis and is particularly associated with devotion to Rama.\nHe contributed significantly to Carnatic music, including the creation/popularization of several ragas.\nAnnamacharya lived much earlier than Tyagaraja, so they were not contemporaries.\nAnnamacharya's compositions are prominently devoted to Lord Venkateswara.","revisionPoints":"Tyagaraja → major Carnatic music composer.\nTyagaraja → associated especially with devotion to Rama.\nAnnamacharya → associated with Venkateswara.\nBoth are important figures in South Indian devotional music."},
{"no":45,"text":"Which are the main features of the Rule of Law?\nLimitation of powers\nEquality before law\nPeople's responsibility to Government\nLiberty and civil rights","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Rule of Law","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Rule of law requires that governmental power is limited by law.\nIt emphasizes equality before the law.\nProtection of liberty and civil rights is an important component.\n\"People's responsibility to Government\" is not a defining feature of rule of law in the source.","revisionPoints":"Rule of law → absence of arbitrary government.\nEquality before law is central.\nGovernment itself is subject to law.\nProtection of fundamental rights supports rule of law."},
{"no":46,"text":"Legal tender means:","A":"Money tendered in courts","B":"Money which a creditor must legally accept in settlement of debts","C":"Bank money like cheques","D":"Metallic money only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Legal Tender","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Legal tender is money recognized by law as valid for settling monetary obligations.\nA creditor generally cannot refuse legally valid tender within the applicable legal limits.\nCheques are not automatically legal tender merely because they are used as payment instruments.","revisionPoints":"Legal tender → legally recognized means of settling debts.\nCurrency notes and coins can constitute legal tender subject to applicable rules.\nCheque → payment instrument, not itself legal tender.\nDistinguish legal tender from broader forms of money/payment."},
{"no":47,"text":"If a commodity is provided free by the Government, then:","A":"Opportunity cost is zero.","B":"Opportunity cost is ignored.","C":"Opportunity cost is transferred from consumers to taxpayers.","D":"Opportunity cost is transferred to Government.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Opportunity Cost","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"\"Free\" to the consumer does not mean that the resource has no cost.\nGovernment must use public resources to provide the commodity.\nUltimately, the cost can be borne through taxation or other public resources.","revisionPoints":"Opportunity cost → value of the next-best alternative forgone.\nGovernment goods/services are not necessarily costless to society.\nTaxpayers ultimately finance many public expenditures.\nAlways distinguish price paid by consumer from economic cost."},
{"no":48,"text":"Increase in real GNP does not necessarily indicate higher economic development if:","A":"Industrial output lags behind agriculture.","B":"Agricultural output lags behind industry.","C":"Poverty and unemployment increase.","D":"Imports exceed exports.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["GNP","Economic Development","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Economic development is broader than growth in national income.\nIf real GNP rises while poverty and unemployment increase, the benefits of growth may not be broadly distributed.\nDevelopment also involves improvements in living standards and social welfare.","revisionPoints":"Economic growth → increase in output/income.\nEconomic development → broader improvement in welfare and living standards.\nPoverty, unemployment and inequality are important development indicators.\nGDP/GNP growth alone does not capture all dimensions of development."},
{"no":49,"text":"Human capital formation refers to a process that increases:\nCapital owned by individuals\nKnowledge, skills and capacities\nTangible wealth\nIntangible wealth","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 4","D":"1, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Human Capital","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Human capital refers to the knowledge, skills, abilities and capacities embodied in people.\nEducation, training and healthcare contribute to human capital formation.\nHuman capital is primarily associated with intangible assets, rather than physical/tangible wealth.","revisionPoints":"Human capital → knowledge + skills + capabilities.\nEducation and vocational training → human capital formation.\nHealth → improves productive capacity.\nHuman capital is an intangible form of economic wealth."},
{"no":50,"text":"Despite high savings, capital formation may not significantly increase because of:","A":"Weak administrative machinery","B":"Illiteracy","C":"High population density","D":"High capital-output ratio","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Capital Formation","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The capital-output ratio indicates the amount of capital required to generate additional output.\nA high ratio means more capital is required to produce a given increase in output.\nTherefore, even high savings may not translate into proportionately high output or capital formation.","revisionPoints":"Capital-output ratio → relationship between capital investment and output.\nHigher ratio → lower efficiency of capital in generating additional output.\nSavings provide resources for investment.\nInvestment efficiency affects economic growth."},
{"no":51,"text":"After the Santhal Uprising, which measures were taken?\nCreation of Santhal Parganas.\nRestriction on transfer of Santhal land to non-Santhals.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Santhal Uprising","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Santhal Uprising of 1855–56 led the colonial administration to introduce special administrative arrangements.\nSanthal Parganas was created as a distinct administrative area.\nRestrictions were also imposed on the transfer of tribal land to outsiders.","revisionPoints":"Santhal Uprising → 1855–56.\nMajor leaders → Sidhu and Kanhu Murmu.\nCauses included exploitation by moneylenders, zamindars and colonial authorities.\nCreation of Santhal Parganas followed the uprising."},
{"no":52,"text":"One major economic result of British rule in the 19th century was:","A":"Increase in handicraft exports","B":"Growth of Indian-owned factories","C":"Commercialization of agriculture","D":"Rapid urbanization","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Commercialization of Agriculture","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"British economic policies encouraged cultivation of crops for the market and export.\nFarmers increasingly produced commercial crops such as indigo, cotton and opium in different regions.\nThis contributed to the commercialization of agriculture.","revisionPoints":"Commercialization → production increasingly oriented toward the market.\nCommercial crops included indigo, cotton, opium and jute in different regions.\nColonial trade policies affected traditional handicrafts.\nCash-crop cultivation could increase vulnerability to market fluctuations."},
{"no":53,"text":"Under Article 356, when President's Rule is imposed:","A":"Assembly is automatically dissolved.","B":"Legislature's powers may be exercised by Parliament.","C":"Article 19 is suspended.","D":"President directly makes laws.","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Article 356","President's Rule","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Article 356 deals with failure of constitutional machinery in a State.\nDuring President's Rule, the powers of the State Legislature may be exercised by Parliament.\nThe State Legislative Assembly is not necessarily automatically dissolved; it may be suspended or dissolved depending on circumstances and parliamentary approval.\nArticle 19 is not automatically suspended merely because President's Rule is imposed.","revisionPoints":"Article 356 → President's Rule.\nBased on Governor's report or otherwise.\nProclamation requires Parliamentary approval within the constitutional time limit.\nParliament may exercise the legislative powers of the State.\nHigh Court powers are not assumed by Parliament/President."},
{"no":54,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nCraft\nHeritage State\n1. Puthukkuli Shawls\nTamil Nadu\n2. Sujni Embroidery\nMaharashtra\n3. Uppada Jamdani Sarees\nKarnataka","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 only","D":"2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Handicrafts","tags":["Handicrafts","GI Tag","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Puthukkuli Shawls → Tamil Nadu.\nSujni Embroidery → Bihar, not Maharashtra.\nUppada Jamdani Sarees → Andhra Pradesh, not Karnataka.","revisionPoints":"Puthukkuli Shawls → Tamil Nadu.\nSujni Embroidery → Bihar.\nUppada Jamdani → Andhra Pradesh.\nHandicrafts and textiles are frequently tested through state–craft matching."},
{"no":55,"text":"In which of the following areas can GPS technology be used?\nMobile phone operations\nBanking operations\nControlling power grids","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["GPS","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"GPS provides accurate positioning and timing information.\nMobile networks can use GPS-based timing and location services.\nFinancial networks can use precise timing for synchronization.\nPower grids can use GPS timing for synchronization and monitoring.","revisionPoints":"GPS → Global Positioning System.\nProvides position, navigation and precise timing.\nApplications extend beyond navigation.\nTelecommunications, finance and power infrastructure can use precise timing."},
{"no":56,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nRBI manages and services Government of India Securities but not State Government Securities.\nTreasury Bills are issued only by the Government of India.\nTreasury Bills are issued at a discount to face value.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Treasury Bills","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"RBI manages government securities for both the Central and State Governments.\nTreasury Bills are short-term instruments issued by the Central Government.\nThey are issued at a discount to face value and redeemed at face value.","revisionPoints":"Government securities → debt instruments issued by governments.\nTreasury Bills: short-term government securities.\nThey are zero-coupon instruments.\nT-Bills are issued at discount and redeemed at face value.\nRBI manages public debt and government securities operations."},
{"no":57,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nEarth's magnetic field has reversed every few hundred thousand years.\nMore than 4 billion years ago Earth's atmosphere had 54% oxygen and no CO₂.\nEarly living organisms modified Earth's atmosphere.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Earth's Magnetic Field","Atmosphere","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Earth's magnetic field has undergone multiple geomagnetic reversals over geological time.\nThe early atmosphere did not contain 54% oxygen with no CO₂ as stated.\nEarly organisms, particularly photosynthetic organisms, significantly altered Earth's atmospheric composition by releasing oxygen.","revisionPoints":"Geomagnetic reversal → reversal of Earth's magnetic polarity.\nEarly Earth atmosphere differed greatly from today's atmosphere.\nPhotosynthetic organisms contributed to atmospheric oxygenation.\nGreat Oxidation Event is associated with a major rise in atmospheric oxygen."},
{"no":58,"text":"\"WannaCry\", \"Petya\" and \"EternalBlue\" are related to:","A":"Exoplanets","B":"Cryptocurrency","C":"Cyber attacks","D":"Mini satellites","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Cyber Attacks","Ransomware","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"WannaCry and Petya are associated with major malware/ransomware attacks.\nEternalBlue is an exploit associated with vulnerabilities in Microsoft's SMB protocol.\nThese became prominent examples of cybersecurity threats.","revisionPoints":"WannaCry → ransomware outbreak.\nPetya → malware/ransomware family.\nEternalBlue → exploit targeting SMB vulnerability.\nCybersecurity threats include malware, ransomware, phishing and exploits."},
{"no":59,"text":"Which of the following fall under Conservation Agriculture?\nAvoiding monoculture\nMinimum tillage\nAvoiding plantation crops\nUsing crop residues as mulch\nCrop rotation","A":"1, 3 and 4","B":"2, 3, 4 and 5","C":"2, 4 and 5","D":"1, 2, 3 and 5","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Conservation Agriculture","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Minimum tillage reduces soil disturbance.\nCrop residues can be retained as mulch to protect the soil.\nCrop rotation improves soil health and helps manage pests and nutrient depletion.\nAvoiding monoculture and plantation crops are not defining principles listed by the source.","revisionPoints":"Conservation agriculture emphasizes:\nminimum soil disturbance,\nsoil cover,\ncrop diversification.\nCrop residues can help conserve soil moisture.\nCrop rotation can improve nutrient cycling.\nReduced tillage can reduce soil erosion."},
{"no":60,"text":"The term \"Sixth Mass Extinction\" is associated with:","A":"Monoculture farming","B":"Asteroid collision","C":"GM crops","D":"Human-induced biodiversity loss due to habitat destruction, pollution and climate change","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Sixth Mass Extinction","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The term refers to the ongoing large-scale loss of biodiversity attributed largely to human activities.\nMajor pressures include habitat destruction, overexploitation, pollution, invasive species and climate change.\nIt is distinguished from past mass extinctions caused primarily by natural geological or astronomical events.","revisionPoints":"Biodiversity loss → major environmental concern.\nMajor drivers:\nhabitat destruction,\noverexploitation,\ninvasive species,\npollution,\nclimate change.\nMass extinction → extinction occurring at unusually high rates across many groups.\nHuman activity is a major modern driver of species decline.\nUPSC – Questions 61–80"},
{"no":61,"text":"Regarding IRNSS (NavIC):\nThree satellites are in geostationary orbit and four in geosynchronous orbit.\nIt covers India and about 1500 km beyond its borders.\nIt would provide global coverage by 2019.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["NavIC","IRNSS","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"IRNSS, now known as NavIC, was designed as a regional navigation satellite system.\nIts planned constellation included satellites in geostationary and geosynchronous orbits.\nIts service area extends over India and approximately 1,500 km beyond India's borders.\nIt was not designed to provide global coverage by 2019.","revisionPoints":"IRNSS → NavIC.\nNavIC is India's regional satellite navigation system.\nProvides positioning, navigation and timing services.\nDesigned primarily for India and the surrounding region."},
{"no":62,"text":"Which of the following are predictions of Einstein's General Theory of Relativity?\nLight is affected by gravity.\nThe Universe is expanding.\nMatter warps space-time.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["General Theory of Relativity","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"General Relativity describes gravity as the effect of curvature of spacetime.\nMatter and energy influence the geometry of spacetime.\nLight follows curved paths in curved spacetime, producing phenomena such as gravitational lensing.\nGeneral Relativity also provides the theoretical framework for cosmological models involving an expanding universe.","revisionPoints":"Einstein's General Relativity → 1915.\nGravity is described through spacetime curvature.\nMassive objects can bend the path of light.\nImportant predictions/applications include gravitational lensing and gravitational waves."},
{"no":63,"text":"Regarding GM Mustard developed in India:\nIt contains genes providing resistance to certain herbicides.\nIt contains genes facilitating hybrid seed production.\nIt was jointly developed by IARI and Punjab Agricultural University.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["GM Mustard","GM Crops","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The source identifies the hybrid seed production function as the correct statement.\nThe GM mustard technology discussed was developed at Delhi University's Centre for Genetic Manipulation of Crop Plants, rather than jointly by IARI and Punjab Agricultural University.\nThe source does not accept the herbicide-resistance statement in this context.","revisionPoints":"GM → genetically modified.\nGM mustard is associated with development of hybrid mustard technology.\nHybrid production can exploit heterosis.\nAgricultural biotechnology can involve genetic modification for different traits."},
{"no":64,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nTerm\nContext\n1. Belle II\nArtificial Intelligence\n2. Blockchain\nDigital/Cryptocurrency\n3. CRISPR-Cas9\nParticle Physics","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Blockchain","Particle Physics","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Blockchain is associated with distributed digital ledgers and cryptocurrencies.\nBelle II is a particle-physics experiment studying B mesons and related phenomena.\nCRISPR-Cas9 is a gene-editing technology in molecular biology.","revisionPoints":"Belle II → particle physics.\nBlockchain → distributed ledger technology.\nCRISPR-Cas9 → gene editing.\nInterdisciplinary science terms are frequently used in UPSC technology questions."},
{"no":65,"text":"\"Carbon fertilization\" refers to:","A":"Increased plant growth due to higher atmospheric CO₂","B":"Rise in Earth's temperature","C":"Ocean acidification","D":"Adaptation of living beings","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Carbon Fertilization","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Carbon fertilization refers to the effect of increased atmospheric CO₂ concentration on plant photosynthesis and growth.\nCO₂ is a raw material required for photosynthesis.\nThe effect varies among plant species and depends on nutrients, water and environmental conditions.","revisionPoints":"CO₂ → raw material for photosynthesis.\nHigher CO₂ can enhance photosynthesis in some plants.\nThe response is not unlimited.\nNutrient and water availability can restrict the carbon-fertilization effect."},
{"no":66,"text":"The smart home and connected devices scenario described is an example of:","A":"Border Gateway Protocol","B":"Internet of Things (IoT)","C":"Internet Protocol","D":"Virtual Private Network","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Internet of Things","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"IoT refers to interconnected physical devices that collect, communicate and sometimes process data.\nSmart appliances, sensors, cameras and connected home systems are common examples.\nThese devices can communicate over networks and be remotely monitored or controlled.","revisionPoints":"IoT → Internet of Things.\nCombines physical devices, sensors, networks and software.\nExamples → smart homes, connected vehicles and industrial systems.\nIoT generates large amounts of machine-generated data."},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIndia is the third largest manufacturer of silicon wafers used in photovoltaic cells.\nSolar power tariffs are determined by the Solar Energy Corporation of India (SECI).","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Solar Energy","SECI","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The source does not support India's position as the third-largest manufacturer of silicon wafers.\nSECI is an important renewable-energy implementing agency, but solar tariffs are not universally determined by SECI.\nTariff determination can involve regulatory commissions and competitive bidding mechanisms.","revisionPoints":"SECI: Solar Energy Corporation of India.\nRenewable-energy projects can use competitive bidding.\nSolar tariffs can be discovered through auctions/bids.\nSolar photovoltaic technology converts sunlight into electricity."},
{"no":68,"text":"The staple export commodities of the English East India Company from Bengal in the mid-18th century were:","A":"Raw cotton, oil-seeds and opium","B":"Sugar, salt, zinc and lead","C":"Copper, silver, gold, spices and tea","D":"Cotton, silk, saltpetre and opium","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["East India Company Trade","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Bengal was a major centre of textile production and other commercially valuable commodities.\nThe East India Company exported products including cotton textiles, silk, saltpetre and opium.\nBengal's commercial importance contributed significantly to British economic interests.","revisionPoints":"Bengal → major centre of textile production.\nSilk and cotton textiles were important exports.\nSaltpetre was strategically important because of its use in gunpowder.\nEast India Company trade gradually became linked with political control."},
{"no":69,"text":"A major significance of the Champaran Satyagraha was:","A":"Active participation of lawyers, students and women","B":"Participation of Dalits and Tribals","C":"Integration of peasant grievances into the National Movement","D":"Decline in plantation crops","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Champaran Satyagraha","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Champaran Satyagraha of 1917 focused on the problems of indigo cultivators.\nGandhi intervened in a local agrarian issue through Satyagraha.\nIt helped connect peasant grievances with the broader Indian national movement.","revisionPoints":"Champaran Satyagraha → 1917.\nGandhi's first major Satyagraha in India.\nAssociated with indigo cultivation and the tinkathia system.\nDemonstrated the use of non-violent mass mobilisation in agrarian issues."},
{"no":70,"text":"Who were the founders of the Hind Mazdoor Sabha (1948)?","A":"B. Krishna Pillai, E.M.S. Namboodiripad and K.C. George","B":"Jayaprakash Narayan, Deendayal Upadhyaya and M.N. Roy","C":"C.P. Ramaswamy Iyer, K. Kamaraj and Veeresalingam Pantulu","D":"Ashok Mehta, T.S. Ramanujam and G.G. Mehta","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Hind Mazdoor Sabha","Trade Unions","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Hind Mazdoor Sabha (HMS) was established in 1948.\nIt emerged as an important trade-union organisation.\nThe founders listed in the source are Ashok Mehta, T.S. Ramanujam and G.G. Mehta.","revisionPoints":"HMS → established in 1948.\nIt is a major Indian trade-union organisation.\nTrade unions played an important role in India's labour movement.\nPost-independence India saw the growth of multiple trade-union federations."},
{"no":71,"text":"The \"Sthanakvasi\" sect belongs to:","A":"Buddhism","B":"Jainism","C":"Vaishnavism","D":"Shaivism","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Jainism","Sthanakvasi","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Sthanakvasi is a reformist sect within Jainism.\nIt is associated with the Shvetambara tradition.\nSthanakvasis generally reject temple worship and emphasize meditation and spiritual discipline.","revisionPoints":"Sthanakvasi → Jainism.\nAssociated with Shvetambara Jainism.\nJainism has two major traditions → Digambara and Shvetambara.\nSthanakvasi tradition emphasizes non-idolatrous worship."},
{"no":72,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nWhite marble was used in Buland Darwaza and Khanqah at Fatehpur Sikri.\nRed sandstone and marble were used in Bara Imambara and Rumi Darwaza, Lucknow.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mughal Architecture","Fatehpur Sikri","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Buland Darwaza is primarily constructed using red sandstone, with marble used for decorative elements.\nBara Imambara and Rumi Darwaza are primarily associated with Lakhauri bricks and lime-based construction, rather than the material combination stated.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Buland Darwaza → Fatehpur Sikri.\nFatehpur Sikri → associated with Akbar.\nBara Imambara → Lucknow.\nRumi Darwaza → Lucknow's historic architectural landmark."},
{"no":73,"text":"Which foreign traveller elaborately discussed the diamond mines of India?","A":"Francois Bernier","B":"Jean-Baptiste Tavernier","C":"Jean de Thevenot","D":"Abbe Barthelemy Carre","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Foreign Travellers","Golconda","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Jean-Baptiste Tavernier was a French traveller and jeweller.\nHe travelled extensively in India and recorded detailed observations about its diamond mines and trade.\nHis accounts are valuable sources for understanding aspects of Mughal-era commerce.","revisionPoints":"Tavernier → French traveller and jeweller.\nVisited India several times.\nDescribed Indian diamond mines and the gem trade.\nForeign travellers are important sources for medieval Indian history."},
{"no":74,"text":"According to Indian history, who is the future Buddha yet to come?","A":"Avalokiteshvara","B":"Lokesvara","C":"Maitreya","D":"Padmapani","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhism","Maitreya","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Maitreya is regarded in Buddhist tradition as the Buddha who will appear in the future.\nHe is associated with the future renewal of Buddhist teaching.","revisionPoints":"Maitreya → future Buddha.\nAvalokiteshvara → Bodhisattva associated with compassion.\nPadmapani → a form/representation associated with Avalokiteshvara.\nBodhisattva concepts are particularly important in Mahayana Buddhism."},
{"no":75,"text":"Which statement does not apply to Lord Wellesley's Subsidiary Alliance?","A":"Maintaining a large standing army at another's expense","B":"Keeping India safe from Napoleonic danger","C":"Securing a fixed income for the Company","D":"Establishing British paramountcy over Indian States","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Subsidiary Alliance","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Subsidiary Alliance required Indian rulers to accept the presence of British troops.\nThe ruler had to bear the cost of maintaining the subsidiary force.\nIt strengthened British political and military control over Indian states.\nA guaranteed fixed income for the Company was not itself the defining objective of the system.","revisionPoints":"Subsidiary Alliance → associated with Lord Wellesley.\nIndian rulers accepted British troops within their territories.\nRulers generally surrendered independent control over foreign relations.\nSystem expanded British political influence over princely states."},
{"no":76,"text":"Which of the following led to the introduction of English education in India?\nCharter Act, 1813\nGeneral Committee of Public Instruction (1823)\nOrientalist-Anglicist Controversy","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["English Education","Charter Act 1813","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Charter Act of 1813 allocated funds for education.\nThe General Committee of Public Instruction was established in 1823.\nThe Orientalist-Anglicist controversy influenced the debate over the language and character of education.\nThese developments contributed to the eventual expansion of English education.","revisionPoints":"Charter Act → 1813.\nGeneral Committee of Public Instruction → 1823.\nOrientalist vs Anglicist debate → language and educational priorities.\nMacaulay's Minute, 1835 strongly favoured English education."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which of the following is an artificial lake?","A":"Kodaikanal (Tamil Nadu)","B":"Kolleru (Andhra Pradesh)","C":"Nainital (Uttarakhand)","D":"Renuka (Himachal Pradesh)","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Artificial Lakes","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Kodaikanal Lake is a man-made/artificial lake.\nKolleru is a natural freshwater lake.\nNainital is a natural lake.\nRenuka is a natural lake.","revisionPoints":"Kodaikanal Lake → Tamil Nadu.\nKolleru → natural freshwater lake in Andhra Pradesh.\nNainital → Uttarakhand.\nRenuka → Himachal Pradesh.\nUPSC frequently tests natural vs artificial lakes."},
{"no":78,"text":"Regarding Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY):\nIt is the flagship scheme of the Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship.\nIt imparts soft skills, entrepreneurship, financial and digital literacy.\nIt aligns competencies to the National Skills Qualification Framework (NSQF).","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["PMKVY","Skill Development","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"PMKVY is a flagship skill-development initiative.\nIt focuses on skill training and employability.\nTraining can include soft skills and other supporting competencies.\nQualifications are aligned with the NSQF framework.","revisionPoints":"PMKVY → Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana.\nMinistry → Skill Development and Entrepreneurship.\nFocus → skill development and employability.\nNSQF → provides a framework for competency-based qualifications."},
{"no":79,"text":"Which organization changed its name to \"Swarajya Sabha\" in 1920?","A":"All India Home Rule League","B":"Hindu Mahasabha","C":"South Indian Liberal Federation","D":"Servants of India Society","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Home Rule Movement","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The All India Home Rule League was associated with the Home Rule movement.\nIn 1920, it changed its name to Swarajya Sabha.\nThe movement had earlier been associated particularly with Annie Besant and Bal Gangadhar Tilak.","revisionPoints":"Home Rule Movement → 1916.\nMajor leaders → Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Annie Besant.\nObjective → self-government for India.\nAll India Home Rule League → later renamed Swarajya Sabha."},
{"no":80,"text":"Which event happened earliest?","A":"Arya Samaj founded","B":"Dinabandhu Mitra wrote Neel Darpan","C":"Bankim Chandra wrote Anandamath","D":"Satyendranath Tagore entered ICS","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["19th Century Bengal","Chronology","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Neel Darpan was written by Dinabandhu Mitra in the early 1860s.\nArya Samaj was founded in 1875.\nAnandamath was published later, in the 1880s.\nSatyendranath Tagore entered the ICS in 1863, after Neel Darpan.","revisionPoints":"Neel Darpan → associated with the Indigo Revolt.\nArya Samaj → founded by Swami Dayanand Saraswati in 1875.\nAnandamath → Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay.\nSatyendranath Tagore → first Indian to successfully enter the ICS."},
{"no":81,"text":"Heavy sand mining in riverbeds can lead to:\nDecreased salinity\nPollution of groundwater\nLowering of the water table","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Resources","tags":["Sand Mining","Groundwater","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Excessive sand extraction can disturb the natural structure of riverbeds.\nIt can affect groundwater recharge and increase the risk of groundwater pollution.\nRemoval of riverbed material can contribute to a lowering of the water table.\nThe source does not identify decreased salinity as a consequence.","revisionPoints":"River sand is important for maintaining riverbed and aquifer systems.\nExcessive sand mining can alter river morphology.\nPossible impacts include groundwater depletion and ecosystem disturbance.\nSustainable extraction is important for riverine ecosystems."},
{"no":82,"text":"Regarding agricultural soils:\nOrganic matter reduces water-holding capacity.\nSoil plays no role in sulphur cycle.\nIrrigation can lead to soil salinization.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Soil Salinization","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Organic matter generally improves soil structure and water-holding capacity, so Statement 1 is incorrect.\nSoil organisms participate in nutrient cycles, including the sulphur cycle, making Statement 2 incorrect.\nImproper irrigation and inadequate drainage can cause salt accumulation in soil.","revisionPoints":"Soil organic matter → improves soil structure and moisture retention.\nSoil microorganisms participate in nutrient cycling.\nIrrigation without adequate drainage → risk of salinization.\nSalinity can reduce crop productivity."},
{"no":83,"text":"Partnership for Action on Green Economy (PAGE) emerged at:","A":"Johannesburg Summit (2002)","B":"Rio+20 Conference (2012)","C":"Paris Climate Conference (2015)","D":"World Sustainable Development Summit (2016)","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["PAGE","Rio+20","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"PAGE emerged from the Rio+20 Conference in 2012.\nIt supports countries in transitioning towards inclusive and environmentally sustainable economies.","revisionPoints":"Rio+20 → United Nations Conference on Sustainable Development, 2012.\nPAGE → Partnership for Action on Green Economy.\nFocus → green economy and sustainable development.\nConnects economic transformation with environmental sustainability."},
{"no":84,"text":"3D Printing has applications in:\nConfectionery\nBionic ears\nAutomobile industry\nReconstructive surgery\nData processing","A":"1, 3 and 4 only","B":"2, 3 and 5 only","C":"1 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["3D Printing","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"3D printing can be used to manufacture customized objects and components.\nIt has applications in food/confectionery.\nIt is used in automobile manufacturing and prototyping.\nMedical applications include customized implants and reconstructive applications.\nData processing itself is not a direct 3D-printing application.","revisionPoints":"3D printing → additive manufacturing.\nMaterial is built layer by layer.\nApplications → manufacturing, medicine, aerospace and automotive sectors.\nMedical applications include customized prosthetics and implants."},
{"no":85,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nBarren Island volcano is India's only active volcano.\nIt lies about 140 km east of Great Nicobar.\nIt last erupted in 1991 and has remained inactive.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Barren Island","Volcanism","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Barren Island is India's only confirmed active volcano.\nIt is located in the Andaman Sea, not 140 km east of Great Nicobar.\nIt has experienced eruptions after 1991, so Statement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Barren Island → Andaman and Nicobar Islands.\nIndia's only confirmed active volcano.\nLocated in the Andaman Sea.\nThe Andaman-Nicobar region lies in an active tectonic zone."},
{"no":86,"text":"Why is Prosopis juliflora often in the news?","A":"Cosmetic uses","B":"It reduces biodiversity where it spreads.","C":"Used for pesticides","D":"None of the above","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Invasive Species","Prosopis Juliflora","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Prosopis juliflora is an invasive plant species in many regions.\nIt can spread rapidly and compete with native vegetation.\nDense invasion can alter ecosystems and reduce native biodiversity.","revisionPoints":"Prosopis juliflora → invasive plant.\nCan compete with native species for resources.\nInvasive species are a major threat to biodiversity.\nInvasive plants can alter habitat structure and ecosystem processes."},
{"no":87,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nMost coral reefs are found in tropical waters.\nMore than one-third of coral reefs occur in Australia, Indonesia and the Philippines.\nCoral reefs host more animal phyla than tropical rainforests.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Coral Reefs","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Most coral reefs occur in warm tropical and subtropical waters.\nAustralia, Indonesia and the Philippines contain a very significant share of global coral reefs.\nCoral reefs support extremely high marine biodiversity and a wide range of animal groups.","revisionPoints":"Coral reefs generally require warm, shallow, clear waters.\nMajor reef regions → Indo-Pacific and Caribbean.\nGreat Barrier Reef → Australia.\nCoral reefs are among the most biodiverse marine ecosystems.\nThreats include warming, ocean acidification, pollution and destructive fishing."},
{"no":88,"text":"\"Momentum for Change: Climate Neutral Now\" was launched by:","A":"IPCC","B":"UNEP","C":"UNFCCC Secretariat","D":"WMO","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UNFCCC","Climate Neutral Now","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Climate Neutral Now is an initiative associated with the UNFCCC Secretariat.\nIt encourages organizations and individuals to measure, reduce and compensate for greenhouse-gas emissions.","revisionPoints":"UNFCCC → United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change.\nUNFCCC Secretariat supports international climate negotiations.\nClimate neutrality involves reducing emissions and addressing remaining emissions.\nDistinguish UNFCCC from IPCC, which assesses climate science."},
{"no":89,"text":"Match the institution with founder:\nSanskrit College at Banaras — William Jones\nCalcutta Madrasa — Warren Hastings\nFort William College — Arthur Wellesley","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Colonial Education Institutions","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Calcutta Madrasa was established by Warren Hastings in 1781.\nSanskrit College at Banaras is associated with Jonathan Duncan, not William Jones.\nFort William College was established by Lord Wellesley, not Arthur Wellesley.","revisionPoints":"Calcutta Madrasa → 1781, Warren Hastings.\nFort William College → 1800, Lord Wellesley.\nBanaras Sanskrit College → associated with Jonathan Duncan.\nColonial educational institutions were also established to train and administer officials."},
{"no":90,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nRegion\nCountry\n1. Catalonia\nSpain\n2. Crimea\nHungary\n3. Mindanao\nPhilippines\n4. Oromia\nNigeria","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Conflicts","tags":["Separatist Regions","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Catalonia → Spain.\nMindanao → Philippines.\nCrimea is associated with Ukraine/Russia, not Hungary.\nOromia is a regional state of Ethiopia, not Nigeria.","revisionPoints":"Catalonia → northeastern Spain.\nCrimea → Black Sea region.\nMindanao → southern Philippines.\nOromia → Ethiopia.\nRegional geography can be tested through politically significant territories."},
{"no":91,"text":"Arrange the following chronologically:\nFirst democratically elected Communist Government in a State\nImperial Bank renamed State Bank of India\nAir India nationalized\nGoa became part of India","A":"4-1-2-3","B":"3-2-1-4","C":"4-2-1-3","D":"3-1-2-4","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Post-Independence History","Chronology","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Air India nationalized → 1953.\nImperial Bank was transformed into the State Bank of India → 1955.\nFirst democratically elected Communist government in a State was formed in Kerala → 1957.\nGoa became part of India → 1961.","revisionPoints":"Air India nationalization → 1953.\nSBI established → 1955.\nCommunist government in Kerala → 1957.\nGoa liberation/integration → 1961.\nChronology questions require identifying the exact year of each event."},
{"no":92,"text":"Right to Privacy is protected as an intrinsic part of Right to Life and Personal Liberty because of:","A":"Article 14 and 42nd Amendment","B":"Article 17 and DPSPs","C":"Article 21 and the freedoms guaranteed under Part III","D":"Article 24 and 44th Amendment","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Privacy","Article 21","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The Supreme Court has recognized privacy as a fundamental right.\nIt is linked to the constitutional protection of life and personal liberty under Article 21.\nThe right also draws support from the broader framework of fundamental freedoms in Part III.","revisionPoints":"Article 21: protection of life and personal liberty.\nPrivacy was affirmed as a fundamental right by the Supreme Court in K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017).\nFundamental Rights are contained in Part III.\nPrivacy is connected with dignity, autonomy and personal liberty."},
{"no":93,"text":"The Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs announced the Minimum Support Price (MSP) for which of the following?\nArecanut\nBarley\nCoffee\nFinger Millet\nGroundnut\nSesame\nTurmeric","A":"1, 2, 3 and 7 only","B":"2, 4, 5 and 6 only","C":"1, 3, 4, 5 and 6 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["MSP","Crops","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Barley is covered under MSP.\nFinger millet (ragi) is covered under MSP.\nGroundnut and sesame are also among MSP crops.\nArecanut, coffee and turmeric are not included in the MSP list in the source.","revisionPoints":"MSP is announced for a set of major agricultural commodities.\nMSP covers cereals, pulses, oilseeds and certain commercial crops.\nBarley → cereal.\nRagi → millet.\nGroundnut and sesame → oilseeds."},
{"no":94,"text":"Pakhui Wildlife Sanctuary is located in:","A":"Arunachal Pradesh","B":"Manipur","C":"Meghalaya","D":"Nagaland","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Pakke Tiger Reserve","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Pakhui Wildlife Sanctuary is located in Arunachal Pradesh.\nIt is now known as Pakke Tiger Reserve.\nIt is an important biodiversity area of the Eastern Himalayas.","revisionPoints":"Pakhui/Pakke → Arunachal Pradesh.\nNow known as Pakke Tiger Reserve.\nLocated in the Eastern Himalayan biodiversity region.\nImportant habitat for several forest species."},
{"no":95,"text":"With reference to India's Satellite Launch Vehicles, consider the following statements:\nPSLV launches satellites mainly for Earth observation while GSLV is mainly used for communication satellites.\nSatellites launched by PSLV appear fixed in the sky from Earth.\nGSLV Mk III is a four-stage launch vehicle using solid and liquid stages alternately.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 2","D":"3 only","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["PSLV","GSLV","ISRO","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"PSLV has been extensively used for Earth-observation and polar/sun-synchronous missions.\nSatellites placed by PSLV generally do not appear fixed in the sky because they are usually not geostationary.\nGSLV Mk III, now called LVM3, is a three-stage launch vehicle, not four-stage.","revisionPoints":"PSLV → Polar Satellite Launch Vehicle.\nGSLV → Geosynchronous Satellite Launch Vehicle.\nLVM3 → India's heavy-lift launch vehicle.\nGeostationary satellites appear fixed relative to a point on Earth's equator.\nPolar/sun-synchronous satellites move relative to observers on Earth."},
{"no":96,"text":"Regarding governance of Public Sector Banks:\nGovernment's capital infusion into PSBs steadily increased during the last decade.\nMerger of associate banks with SBI has strengthened PSBs.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Public Sector Banks","Bank Merger","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Government capital infusion into PSBs has not increased steadily every year.\nSeveral recapitalization exercises occurred at different points.\nThe merger of SBI's associate banks was intended to strengthen the bank through greater scale and operational integration.","revisionPoints":"PSBs → Public Sector Banks.\nGovernment recapitalization can strengthen bank capital.\nSBI merged its associate banks in 2017.\nBank consolidation can aim at economies of scale and stronger balance sheets."},
{"no":97,"text":"Which of the following are exempted under GST?\nCereal grains hulled\nChicken eggs cooked\nFish processed and canned\nNewspapers containing advertisements","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["GST","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The source identifies hulled cereal grains as exempt.\nCooked chicken eggs are included among the exempt items in the question.\nNewspapers remain exempt even when they contain advertisements.\nProcessed and canned fish is taxable, making Statement 3 incorrect.","revisionPoints":"GST → Goods and Services Tax.\nIt is a destination-based indirect tax.\nGST exemptions vary according to the applicable classification and notifications.\nAlways distinguish exempt goods from zero-rated supplies."},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe definition of \"Critical Wildlife Habitat\" is incorporated in the Forest Rights Act, 2006.\nFor the first time in India, Baigas have been given Habitat Rights.\nMinistry of Environment decides and declares Habitat Rights for PVTGs.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Critical Wildlife Habitat","Forest Rights Act","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"The concept of Critical Wildlife Habitat is incorporated into the Forest Rights Act, 2006.\nThe Baiga community was the first Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) to receive recognition of Habitat Rights in the source.\nHabitat Rights are recognized through the framework of the Forest Rights Act and the appropriate government process, not solely by the Ministry of Environment.","revisionPoints":"FRA → Forest Rights Act, 2006.\nPVTG → Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group.\nHabitat Rights are distinct from ordinary individual forest rights.\nBaiga → important tribal community associated with the recognition of Habitat Rights."},
{"no":99,"text":"Which of the following spread plant diseases?\nBirds\nDust\nRain\nWind","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Plant Diseases","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"Plant pathogens can be transported by birds.\nWind can carry spores and other pathogens.\nRain splash can spread pathogens from infected to healthy plants.\nDust can carry fungal spores and bacteria.","revisionPoints":"Plant diseases can spread through:\nwind,\nrain splash,\nsoil/dust,\ninsects and animals.\nFungal spores can be dispersed through air.\nVector-borne transmission is also important.\nDisease management includes sanitation, resistant varieties and biological/chemical control."},
{"no":100,"text":"With reference to Organic Farming in India:\nNational Programme for Organic Production (NPOP) is operated under the Ministry of Commerce.\nAPEDA acts as the Secretariat for implementing NPOP.\nSikkim is India's first fully organic State.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Organic Farming","NPOP","Sikkim","UPSC","2018"],"expl":"NPOP is implemented under the Ministry of Commerce & Industry.\nAPEDA serves as the Secretariat for implementing NPOP.\nSikkim became India's first fully organic State in 2016.","revisionPoints":"NPOP → National Programme for Organic Production.\nAPEDA → Agricultural and Processed Food Products Export Development Authority.\nNPOP is important for organic certification and exports.\nSikkim → India's first fully organic State, declared in 2016."},
];
